## "Let's think step by step"

<At levels="simple">

A model writes one token at a time, and every token it writes becomes part of what it reads next (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />).
If it has to answer straight away, it gets no room to work. If it writes its working out first,
a <GlossaryPopover term="chain-of-thought">chain of thought</GlossaryPopover>, each step can build on
the last. Reasoning models are trained to do this (<ChapterRef to="reasoning-models" />); with other models, you can ask.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A model writes one token at a time, and every token it writes becomes context for the next (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />).
If it has to give the answer as its very first token, all the working has to happen inside one
forward pass. If it writes the working out first, a <GlossaryPopover term="chain-of-thought">chain of thought</GlossaryPopover>,
each step can build on the ones before. Reasoning models are trained to do this (<ChapterRef to="reasoning-models" />); with other
models, you can ask.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The same work for every token

Each token gets one pass through the network, and the pass is the same size every time: for
Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, 28 layers and about 3 billion operations, whether the token is "the" or the
answer to a hard sum.<Cite id="qwen2024" /> Asked for just the answer, it wrote 1 to 4 tokens for
each puzzle below. Asked to think step by step, it wrote between 66 and 427, and each of those
passes could read all the working written before it. Writing out steps buys the model more
computation, spent where the next step can use it.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

Asking works on large models. Adding "Let's think step by step" raised one large model's score on
a set of arithmetic word problems from 17.7% to 78.7%.<Cite id="kojima2022" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Asking works on large models. Showing a few worked examples improved math word problems,<Cite id="wei2022" />
and simply adding "Let's think step by step" raised one large model's accuracy on a set of arithmetic
word problems from 17.7% to 78.7%.<Cite id="kojima2022" />

</At>

## A small model, three ways

Here are the puzzles from <ChapterRef to="reasoning-models" />, put to Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct with three different endings:

<StepsTally />

<At levels="simple">

For this small model, the steps didn't help overall: each way got 4 of the 6 right, but not the
same 4. Asked for just the answer, it fell for the trap and said the ball costs 10 cents. Thinking
step by step fixed one puzzle and got another wrong. Small models can copy the form of reasoning
without getting it right.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

For this small model, the steps didn't raise the score: each way got 4 of the 6 right, but not the
same 4. Asked for just the answer, it fell for the classic trap and said the ball costs 10 cents.
Thinking step by step fixed the apples but talked itself into "0.2 minutes" for the widgets. Big
gains from step-by-step prompting were found in much larger models; small ones can follow the form
without the reasoning holding up.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Asking more than once

The three endings got different puzzles wrong, and every puzzle was right under at least one of
them. Taking the answer that most of the three replies agree on would have scored 5 of 6. That's a
rough version of *self-consistency*: sample several chains of thought for the same prompt and keep
the most common final answer. On a set of grade-school math problems it raised a large model's
accuracy by 17.9 points over a single greedy chain. The idea is that a hard problem has many
paths to its one right answer, while wrong paths tend to end in different places.<Cite id="wang2023" />

</At>

## Formats are for programs

<At levels="simple">

The last ending asked for the answer on a final line, "Answer: …", so that a program could read
it. The model did that only once in six tries. So a program reading the replies has to check the
format, and ask again when it wasn't followed.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The last ending asked for the answer on a final line, "Answer: …", so that a program could read it.
The model did that only once in six tries. A program that reads the replies can't assume the
format was followed: it has to check, and ask again or fall back when it wasn't. Asking for a format
makes the reply easier to use, not guaranteed.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Making the format certain

When the program runs the model itself, it can do more than ask. At each step, it can set the
probability of every token that would break the format to zero, rescale the rest, and pick from
what's left. A format written as a regular expression or a grammar can be turned into an index of
the allowed tokens at each point in the reply, so this adds little time.<Cite id="willard2023" />
The format is then guaranteed. The answer inside it isn't: the model still picks among the allowed
tokens by their probabilities, right or wrong.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Writing the working out gives each step something to build on; it helped large models a lot.
- On a small model the effect was mixed: the same score, with different mistakes.
- Ask for a fixed format when a program reads the reply, and check that it was followed.

<At levels="deep">

- Each token gets the same fixed computation, so written steps buy more; voting over several
  replies and limiting the allowed tokens help further.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** if wording matters this much, how much can harmless changes move the answer?
